c-96d8-4927-98c8-002f79540e88","position":{"commit_sha":"281a2ff8d8771e8c9e842b08311ef31a437c2d4e","position":"1787399254095926001-a7ff9a8c-96d8-4927-98c8-002f79540e88"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"84befc13e0853b5fdc3a35e3f8db557ee576d99c7b849e0c69bcc8f456a76f26","event_uuid":"2b1dd8c3-303e-48df-9db4-e636ff1fa00a","position":{"commit_sha":"127b80e9e84e280cc50edeb1941c448eb4687d58","position":"1787399944449788001-2b1dd8c3-303e-48df-9db4-e636ff1fa00a"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"74e4107f17844e0e0e33c3ae9ce1cf4e73c9e68a61db2935923c02b37032cd88","event_uuid":"3a78360c-6e8c-48e5-b965-d3a10de0c880","position":{"commit_sha":"7a0914eb365d9ec5ffe03d808ac322e125465923","position":"1787399950639741001-3a78360c-6e8c-48e5-b965-d3a10de0c880"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"completion_expectation":"required","created_at":1787081121609742001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"9217834a-7059-4944-bb50-a41237a8c6ad","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"cfb7-f2ce-ce8a-4cfd"},{"link_uuid":"6c974e8c-114f-4c25-a2b6-376578e66fa6","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"17e1-3e00-040f-4002"},{"link_uuid":"211d2f7c-c51c-4271-b864-ecb75af2f15b","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"e3e4-9f3d-e62e-4903"},{"link_uuid":"d1a860be-a69b-4d14-a483-6b746d2691dc","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"1d48-16ae-90ac-4836"}],"description":"## Context / Problem\nAn indeterminate plan review can contain expensive successful calls plus a small retryable coverage gap. A normal rerun repeats everything, while blindly resuming old checkpoints risks applying results to changed material or policy. The maintainer approved an explicit `rebar review-plan --retry` override that resumes only the literal latest eligible review and authorizes a fresh attempt budget.\n\n## Approach\nAdd CLI parsing and a narrow library-internal retry request. Eligibility is read from the latest retained `REVIEW_RESULT` sidecar payload (`sidecar.latest_review_result`): its embedded reducer-ignored `discovery_journal` (built from the Pass-1 `discovery_trace`) enumerates the units of that exact review, each carrying an outcome `kind` from the journal's actual vocabulary **{success, resumed, failed, cancelled}**, and the verdict must be `INDETERMINATE` with at least one retryable missing unit. **The journal is content-free — it is used only to identify the latest review, enumerate its traced units, and classify each as reusable vs missing; it is never the source of finding content.** Reusable units are exactly the journal units with kind `success` or `resumed`; retryable missing units are those with kind `failed` (a `cancelled` unit marks an interrupted review and is likewise treated as missing/to-re-run). The reusable *content* of each `success`/`resumed` unit is reconstructed from the chunk-atomic `CheckpointEnvelope`s written by `sizing.save_checkpoint`: for each such unit the retry recomputes its `checkpoint_identity` / `identity_digest` from the current ticket material, code/ref, effective policy, prompts/contracts, model/mode, topology, context, dependencies, and source, then loads the envelope via `sizing.load_checkpoint(ctx, digest)` and reuses its stored `findings` — exactly the resume join `pass1` already performs. A unit whose recomputed digest matches no stored envelope (changed material/policy/etc.), or whose envelope is absent, is NOT reusable: it is treated as missing and re-run. **Budget-shed criteria emit no journal/trace unit — they are recorded only as `budget_indeterminate` findings in the payload; the retry enumerates the shed set from those recorded findings (not from the journal) and, under the fresh attempt budget, re-attempts them as missing units.** Assembled prior verdict fields (`findings`, `coverage`) come from that same sidecar `build_payload`. This retry layers ONLY a latest-review eligibility gate and a fresh attempt budget over the existing chunk-checkpoint resume seam — it introduces no second resume mechanism. The lineage must be digest-complete and content-identical across ticket material, code/ref, effective policy, prompts/contracts, model/mode, topology, context, dependencies, and source. Legacy `REVIEW_RESULT` payloads without the versioned `discovery_journal`, and legacy (pre-namespace) checkpoints, never seed retry.\n\nReuse every eligible `success`/`resumed` unit's checkpointed findings, including clean and blocking results, and issue model calls only for missing units (`failed`/`cancelled` journal units plus the shed set from `budget_indeterminate` findings). Pass-2 verification and coaching reuse unchanged per-finding successes and run only for newly recovered work; deterministic decision, assembly, signature eligibility, and narrow response rendering rerun over the combined result. Each explicit `--retry` grants a fresh per-invocation attempt budget. Cumulative lineage spend is stored as audit telemetry and never blocks a later explicit retry.\n\nFail before any model call when the latest result is not eligible or when its journal is missing, **stale** (the latest sidecar/journal no longer reflects current lineage: its recomputed identity-digest set no longer matches the present material / effective policy / prompts / contracts / model / mode / topology / context / dependencies / source, so no successful unit's checkpoint can be validly reused), corrupt, legacy, or digest-mismatched. Refusal is not a silent full review and gives the operator the normal full-review remedy. An ineligible/refused retry exits with the established plan-review indeterminate/non-runnable code (exit 2) — the same code a non-runnable `review-plan` already returns — and prints the full-review remedy to stderr; `exit-codes.md` documents this mapping. `--retry` is mutually exclusive with `--force`, `--status`, and `--check`; `--no-sign` remains compatible. Normal review behavior is unchanged without the flag.\n\n`--retry` is a CLI / library operator affordance. The MCP `review_plan` tool mirror is intentionally out of scope for this story (retry is an interactive operator override, not an automated-caller path); the MCP surface keeps full-review semantics and the file impact deliberately excludes the MCP tool module. Retry is operation-linked (CLI plus in-process library) with no CI-provider dependency, so it is portable across environments with any CI provider or none.\n\nDocument the flag in the native canonical `review-plan --help`, generated `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, and `docs/exit-codes.md`. Documentation must explain eligibility, fresh budget, reused blockers/successes, zero-call refusal, incompatible flags, signing behavior, and the distinction from `--status` and `sign-review`. Review and status responses remain narrow end-result views; the retry journal is not printed.\n\n## Migration and Rollback\nThe flag is additive and opt-in. Older journals are deliberately ineligible, so rollout needs no data rewrite. Removing the CLI flag and retry reader restores full-review-only behavior while leaving reducer-ignored journals harmless. Normal review remains the documented fallback for any refusal.\n\n## Testing\nUse a real CLI subprocess with a temporary ticket store plus the production journal codec and stateful fake at the model boundary. Assert exit code, stdout/stderr contract, exact called unit ids, stored lineage, and final verdict. Fault and negative-control cases assert zero calls. A generated-help test proves the documented flag is derived from canonical help rather than hand-maintained drift.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Given a literal latest completed `INDETERMINATE` RP-06 journal with two successful units and one retryable missing unit, `rebar review-plan <id> --retry` calls only the missing unit and combines all three content-identical results.\n- [x] Clean and blocking successes are reusable, budget-shed units remain missing, verification/coaching call only for newly recovered findings, and deterministic decision/assembly reruns over the combined set.\n- [x] Each explicit retry receives the normal fresh per-invocation attempt budget; cumulative lineage usage is recorded but never enforced as a cap.\n- [x] A latest `PASS`/`BLOCK`, a non-retryable indeterminate result, or a missing, legacy, corrupt, stale, or digest-mismatched journal refuses before any model/provider call and never falls through to a full review.\n- [x] CLI contract tests prove `--retry` conflicts with `--force`, `--status`, and `--check`, remains compatible with `--no-sign`, and preserves normal no-flag behavior.\n- [x] Retry output and `review-plan --status` expose only established end-result fields and no per-unit execution trace or verbose journal details.\n- [x] Canonical `review-plan --help`, generated CLI reference, user guide, plan-review gate guide, and exit-code guide document eligibility, fresh budget, reuse/refusal behavior, flag interactions, signing, and the normal-full-review remedy.\n- [x] A real CLI subprocess test proves the eligible resume path, zero-call refusal path, persisted retry lineage, and final exit/verdict semantics end to end.","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py","reason":"Parse, validate, and render the explicit review-plan --retry operation."},{"path":"src/rebar/llm/plan_review/retry.py","reason":"Eligibility, digest validation, lineage reuse, and fresh-budget retry orchestration."},{"path":"src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py","reason":"Route the additive retry request through the normal review entry point and narrow result assembly."},{"path":"src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sidecar.py","reason":"Read and append versioned retry lineage without legacy seeding."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_plan_review_retry.py","reason":"Eligibility, call reuse, fresh budget, flag, signing, refusal, and output contract tests."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_plan_review_cli_retry.py","reason":"Real CLI subprocess and exit-code coverage."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py","reason":"Canonical help and generated-reference drift guard."},{"path":"docs/cli-reference.md","reason":"Generated command reference including --retry."},{"path":"docs/user-guide.md","reason":"Operator workflow and full-review fallback."},{"path":"docs/plan-review-gate.md","reason":"Authoritative eligibility, lineage, signing, and refusal semantics."},{"path":"docs/exit-codes.md","reason":"Retry success, refusal, and transient exit behavior."},{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/_parsers/advanced/llm.py","reason":"Register the review-plan --retry flag and its mutual-exclusion parsing in the advanced LLM CLI parser."},{"path":"src/rebar/llm/workflow/plan_review_recovery.py","reason":"Behavior-preserving module-size extraction of the retry-routing recovery helpers from plan_review/__init__.py to keep it under the 800-LOC cap."},{"path":"tests/unit/test_plan_review_sidecar_heldout.py","reason":"Update the coupled sidecar.emit/build_payload contract test for the additive retry_lineage parameter."}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","managed_refs":[["depends_on","cfb7-f2ce-ce8a-4cfd"],["parent","58bd-d656-c52f-4cf4"],["relates_to","17e1-3e00-040f-4002"],["relates_to","1d48-16ae-90ac-4836"],["relates_to","e3e4-9f3d-e62e-4903"]],"no_file_impact_reason":